bout their contributions to the project.`",{"type":23,"value":119},"Contribution",{"type":23,"value":97},{"type":23,"value":122},"Key elements",{"type":23,"value":124},"Connections",{"type":23,"value":126},"Begin Learning!",{"type":23,"value":128},"This material is for learners with a high level of knowledge. It contains information that is not suitable for beginners!",{"type":23,"value":130},"Need to know",{"type":23,"value":95},{"type":23,"value":133},"Depends on",{"type":23,"value":135},"This material is in development! It may change in the future and even contain errors!",{"type":23,"value":137},"Used by",{"type":23,"value":99},{"type":23,"value":140},"Externals",{"type":23,"value":142},"This is an optional material is for learners who want to dive deeper and gain additional knowledge and context.",{"type":28,"value":144},"(contentTitle) => `Clear and interesting explanation of the topic \"${contentTitle}\". Clear examples, important terms and statements, interesting facts, life applications.`",{"m":146},"function m(number, one, few, includeNumber = true) {\n  const text = number === 1 ? one : few;\n  return includeNumber ? `${number} ${text}` : text;\n}",{"contentPath":148,"mainContent":149},"group/foundations/equations/",{"fullId":150,"shortId":150,"contentRelativePath":151,"title":152,"bookTitle":16,"breadcrumbs":153,"description":156,"stats":157,"connections":162,"contributions":181,"type":186,"children":187},"foundations/equations","01-foundations/02-equations","Equations",[154],{"icon":155,"title":16,"link":18},"book-outline","\n    An overview of equations and equation solving: elementary equations,\n    quadratic equations, and systems of equations.\n    Learn why equations are solved and how they model real-world processes.\n    Includes transformations, domain restrictions, loss of solutions,\n    and extraneous roots.\n  ",{"materials":158,"elements":159},4,{"accent_term":160,"accent_statement":158,"accent_important":160,"problem":161},6,48,{"externals":163},[164,172],{"type":165,"items":166},"own",[167],{"type":168,"title":169,"info":170,"reason":171},"physical","Algebra Word Problems","Anita Harnadek","Huge collection of word problems which lead to an equations with detailed solutions.",{"type":173,"title":16,"items":174},"parent",[175],{"type":176,"title":177,"info":178,"link":179,"reason":180},"web","LibreTexts Mathematics","An open and free online collection of mathematics educational materials.","https://math.libretexts.org/","A large amount of materials and many typical practice problems.",[182],{"contributorId":183,"name":184,"avatarUrl":185},"gwynerva","Peter Radko","/file/contributors/gwynerva/avatar.png","group",[188,271,279],{"type":189,"link":190,"title":191,"description":192,"quickLinks":193,"stats":266},"topic","/article/foundations/equations/elementary/","Elementary Equations","\n    Learn how to solve and transform elementary equations step by step.\n    Simple and visual, with clear examples and without memorizing a bunch of obscure rules.\n    This is a key foundational skill in mathematics and the exact sciences.\n  ",[194,202,209,216,223,230,238,245,252,259],{"link":195,"schemaName":196,"title":197,"quick":198,"seo":199,"description":201},"/article/foundations/equations/elementary/#equality","accent_term","Equality",{},{"title":200},"What is equality?","Two expressions with an equals sign \"=\" between them. Equality can be true (2 = 2) or false (2 = 0).",{"link":203,"schemaName":204,"title":205,"quick":206,"seo":207,"description":208},"/article/foundations/equations/elementary/#equality-false-true","diagram","True and false equality",{},{},"If identical objects stand on the sides of the = sign, then the equality is true. If different -- false.",{"link":210,"schemaName":196,"title":211,"quick":212,"seo":213,"description":215},"/article/foundations/equations/elementary/#equation","Equation",{},{"title":214},"What is an equation?","An equality in which there is one or more unknowns or variables.",{"link":217,"schemaName":196,"title":218,"quick":219,"seo":220,"description":222},"/article/foundations/equations/elementary/#equation-solution","Solving an Equation",{},{"title":221},"What does it mean to solve an equation?","\"Solutions\" or \"roots\" of an equation are numbers that, when substituted for unknowns, turn it into a true equality.\n          \"To solve an equation\" means to find all its roots and prove that there are no other roots.",{"link":224,"schemaName":225,"title":226,"quick":227,"seo":228,"description":229},"/article/foundations/equations/elementary/#same-action-rule","accent_statement","Same Action Rule",{},{},"If the same action is performed on both sides of a true equality, the resulting equality will also be true.",{"link":231,"schemaName":232,"title":233,"quick":234,"seo":235,"description":237},"/article/foundations/equations/elementary/#common-mistakes","list","Mistakes when solving equations",{},{"title":236},"Common mistakes when solving equations","A list of common mistakes that beginners make when solving equations.",{"link":239,"schemaName":240,"title":241,"quick":242,"seo":243,"description":244},"/article/foundations/equations/elementary/#why-solve-equations-1","heading","Why Solve Equations?",{},{},"A set of examples requiring the ability to solve equations to work with.",{"link":246,"schemaName":240,"title":247,"quick":248,"seo":249,"description":251},"/article/foundations/equations/elementary/#triangle-of-formulas","Triangle of Formulas",{},{"title":250},"Magic triangle of formulas","A mnemonic technique for memorizing simple formulas.",{"link":253,"schemaName":240,"title":254,"quick":255,"seo":256,"description":258},"/article/foundations/equations/elementary/#equations-in-life","Equations in Life",{},{"title":257},"Where are equations used in life?","A selection of problems and situations from real life that can be solved if translated into equations.",{"link":260,"schemaName":261,"title":262,"quick":263,"seo":264,"description":265},"/practice/foundations/equations/elementary/#linear-root","problems","Linear equations formula",{},{},"Formula by which you can very quickly find the root of any equation of the form Ax + B = 0.",{"elements":267},{"accent_term":268,"accent_statement":269,"accent_important":158,"problem":270},3,1,36,{"type":186,"link":272,"title":273,"description":274,"stats":275},"/group/foundations/equations/quadratic/","Quadratic Equations","\n    A comprehensive overview of quadratic equations, including quadratic trinomials, completing the square,\n    the discriminant, Vieta’s formulas, factoring, real-life applications, and even methods for solving them mentally.\n  ",{"materials":276,"elements":277},2,{"accent_term":268,"accent_statement":268,"accent_important":276,"problem":278},12,{"type":189,"link":280,"title":281},"/article/foundations/equations/equivalence/","Equivalence of equations",{"contributorsCount":269,"sponsorsCount":283},0,{"shortBookId":5,"frontNav":285},{"type":15,"shortId":5,"title":16,"flags":286,"link":18,"children":287},{},[288],{"type":186,"separator":12,"shortId":150,"title":152,"flags":289,"link":290,"children":291},{},"/group/foundations/equations/",[292,295,310],{"type":189,"shortId":293,"title":191,"flags":294,"link":190},"foundations/equations/elementary",{},{"type":186,"separator":296,"shortId":297,"title":273,"flags":298,"link":272,"children":299},false,"foundations/equations/quadratic",{},[300,305],{"type":189,"shortId":301,"title":302,"flags":303,"link":304},"foundations/equations/quadratic/what-is-it","What is it?",{},"/article/foundations/equations/quadratic/what-is-it/",{"type":189,"shortId":306,"title":307,"flags":308,"link":309},"foundations/equations/quadratic/incomplete","With missing terms",{},"/article/foundations/equations/quadratic/incomplete/",{"type":189,"shortId":311,"title":281,"flags":312,"link":280},"foundations/equations/equivalence",{"dev":12},1770360369179]